The one patch `helpers/build-ripgrep.sh` applies: `ignore` 0.4.23's parallel walker on a target that
is neither `unix` nor `windows`. Applied with `patch -p1` inside the unpacked `ignore-0.4.23.crate`.

Milestone 812 (`std::thread::spawn` runs real threads in one address space) gave `std` threads, so
`available_parallelism` reports the online cores and `rg` picks `ignore`'s parallel walker, whose
`DirEntryRaw::from_entry_os` and `from_path` were placeholders on such a target that answered
"unsupported platform" for every path. They become the `windows` versions without the stored
metadata, which only `windows` keeps, on every such target except wasm, which keeps the stubs
(`wasm32-wasip1` cannot spawn, so a parallel walk past its roots would panic instead). Ruled (a)
by calef on pull request #1892, 2026-10-10 (UTC).
The sequential walker never needed this: it uses `walkdir`.

nife maintains this patch (calef, 2026-10-11 UTC). It is carried until upstream fixes the bug by any
route, and re-checked whenever ripgrep or `ignore` is bumped. The upstream issue: (link pending).

Falsification: attested 2026-10-10 (UTC), on aarch64. With this patch reversed inside the unpacked
`ignore-0.4.23` and `rg` rebuilt, `script/swish-check --arch aarch64` failed on
`/installed/rg needle docs ../hay/secret.txt`, which answered "rg: unsupported platform" twice; with
it applied the same run passed. Not replayable by `script/falsifications --sweep`: that boots
`script/swish-check` against the `rg` already on disk and does not rebuild it. By hand:

    patch -R -p1 -d "$TMPDIR/nife-ripgrep-<hash>/ignore-0.4.23" < helpers/ripgrep-ignore-walk.patch
    NIFE_RIPGREP_HOST=0 NIFE_RIPGREP_TRIPLES=aarch64-unknown-nife helpers/build-ripgrep.sh
    script/swish-check --arch aarch64     # must fail that line
    rm "$TMPDIR/nife-ripgrep-<hash>/ignore-0.4.23/.nife-patch"   # the next build re-patches

